  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nRelatively quiet across the area early this afternoon with \nupper level ridging largely in control. However, there were some\nisolated showers/sprinkles associated with some passing weak \nshortwave energy and low-level moisture convergence. Additional \nshowers and storms moving across southwest into central NE may \nsneak into the forecast area this afternoon, though most short \nterm guidance is in good agreement that they diminish \nsignificantly prior to getting here, so carrying only a 20% \nchance toward the Norfolk/Albion/Columbus area. The main story \ntoday and going forward, however, is the heat. As of noon \ntemperatures were hovering around 90 with dewpoints in the lower\nto mid 70s. This was yielding heat index values approaching 100\nin many locations, with temperatures only expected to increase \nthrough the afternoon. A Heat Advisory will remain in effect for\nmost of the area this afternoon and will continue through \nSaturday for southeast Nebraska and southwest Iowa, with heat \nindex values climbing into the 105-110 range for some spots. \nTemperatures \"cool\" slightly for Sunday and Monday, though still\nlooking at highs in the lower to mid 90s with heat index values\napproaching 100. In addition, overnight lows look to remain in \nthe 70s for most, so not much relief coming with sunset. \n\nOtherwise, we'll see continued on and off shower and storm \nchances the next several days, especially during the overnight \nand early morning hours as the low level jet/moisture transport \npoint toward the area. While we should have a decent amount of \ninstability in place through the weekend and into early next \nweek, any notable deep layer shear looks to remain well to our \nnorth, so any storms that do develop look like they'll be \nunorganized and fairly short-lived (i.e. they'll go up and \nquickly rain themselves out, possibly pushing out some stronger \nwind gusts as they collapse). For tonight/Saturday, latest \nshort-term guidance suggests storms go up in northeast NE or SD \naround 2-3 AM Saturday, and gradually edge southeastward as \noutflow produces gusts of 40-50 mph in our area. While consensus\nsuggests primary storms will initiate over SD, if that shifts \nsouth, the risk for damaging 60+ mph winds and some hail will \nincrease in our area. As it stands, have a 20-45% chance of \nprecip near and north of Norfolk, with a 5% chance of a severe \nstorm. Any gusts should eventually taper off as they approach \nthe Platte River by mid-morning. It'll be worth watching how \nlong any debris clouds linger, as they could have a large impact\non the temperature forecast Saturday.\n\nThen for Sunday into Monday, we'll have a surface boundary that\nparks itself from northwest to southeast across the area that \nwill likely provide another focus for storm development as early\nas Sunday afternoon. However, questions remain on timing, as \nthere could be some lingering morning storms which could delay \nadditional storms until moisture transport picks up in the \nevening/overnight.\n\nWe finally see at least a little relief from the heat toward \nWednesday following the passage of a cold front Tuesday \nafternoon/evening. However, temperatures behind the front look \nto remain in the mid 80s to lower 90s through the remainder of \nthe work week. The front will also bring chances for showers and\nstorms and possibly our highest potential for any severe \nweather through the week, as there could be a little bit better \noverlap of shear and instability, though that window is pretty \nsmall. For the rest of the week, instability looks to be \nlimited, though we will have continued spotty shower and storm \nchances as various bits of shortwave energy slide through and \nmoisture transport points into the area at times. Severe weather\nchances remain low given the lack of instability, but can't \ncompletely rule out something getting organized given the \nincreased shear. Machine learning guidance gives us about a 5% \nchance Wednesday through Friday.",
